    "cefr": "B1",
    "blurbEn": "One of the most admired short stories in world literature opens with a quiet piece of gossip on a seaside promenade. In a single sentence, Chekhov introduces the woman who will quietly upend a man's whole sense of his life.",
    "culturalContextEn": "In the Russian canon, Chekhov is revered as the master who perfected the short story and the modern psychological drama, and \"Дама с собачкой\" (1899) is held up as the supreme example of his prose art. Russian readers and critics celebrate how Chekhov dignifies ordinary life: nothing outwardly dramatic \"happens,\" yet an entire inner transformation unfolds beneath the surface. The story is taught in schools as the embodiment of Chekhov's famous principle of brevity and restraint - his belief that a writer should not explain feelings but show them through small, concrete details. To Russians, Chekhov represents intelligent compassion and the conviction that even quiet, flawed lives deserve serious art.",
