    "culturalContextEn": "\"Кому на Руси жить хорошо\" is celebrated in Russia as Nekrasov's crowning achievement and the great народная (folk) epic of nineteenth-century Russian poetry, written across the 1860s and 1870s in the aftermath of the 1861 emancipation of the serfs. Russians cherish it for taking the poor peasant — not the noble or the hero — as its subject, and for its deliberate imitation of folk song, byliny, and oral speech rhythms rather than refined literary diction. The poem's central question, \"кому живётся весело, вольготно на Руси?\", became a proverbial formula in Russian culture, and Nekrasov is honored in the national tradition as the conscience-stricken \"poet of the people\" whose civic verse made suffering and social injustice the proper material of high art. Schoolchildren still study this Prologue as the gateway to the whole work.",
