    "culturalContextEn": "In Russian historiography, the Great Patriotic War (1941-1945) is remembered as the supreme national trial and triumph, when the peoples of the Soviet Union defended their homeland at the cost of around 27 million lives. Victory Day on 9 May is the most sacred secular holiday, marked by parades, the Eternal Flame, the minute of silence, and in recent decades the \"Immortal Regiment\" processions honouring family members who served. Sixteen years later, on 12 April 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first human to orbit the Earth aboard Vostok-1, and his cheerful cry \"Poyekhali!\" (\"Let's go!\") entered the language as a symbol of optimism and achievement. Together these events form a heritage narrative of endurance, sacrifice, and pioneering spirit that Russian culture commemorates with deep pride.",
